```

Minimal boot waves (open a file passed on the command line):

```java
@Override
protected List<Wave> postBootWaveList() {
    final String path = getParameters().getNamed().get("file");
    if (path == null) {
        return Collections.emptyList();
    }
    return Collections.singletonList(
        WBuilder.of(MyWaves.DO_OPEN)
            .data(WBuilder.waveData(MyWaves.FILE, new File(path)))
            .build());
}
```

- `postBootWaveList()` — waves processed after the first Model is attached; safe to touch Application APIs.
- `getParameters().getNamed()` — reads `--file=...` style CLI arguments from JavaFX.
- `WBuilder.of(...).data(...).build()` — builds a wave you can chain with other boot tasks.


### Default key shortcuts

The AbstractApplication class is using two defaults hotkey:

```java
@Override
protected KeyCode fullScreenKeyCode() {
    return KeyCode.F11; // default is F10; return null to disable
}

@Override
protected KeyCode iconifiedKeyCode() {
    return null; // disable minimize shortcut
}
```

- `fullScreenKeyCode()` — hotkey toggling fullscreen (default **F10**); return `null` to disable.
- `iconifiedKeyCode()` — hotkey iconifying the stage (default **F11**); return `null` to disable.
- Overrides apply globally to the primary stage created by the Application class.


### Exception Manager

JRebirth creates its own uncaught exception handlers in order to log exceptions that were not caught by application code.

It's possible to customize them by overriding methods listed hereafter:

- Exception handler of the JavaFX Application Thread

```java
@Override
protected UncaughtExceptionHandler getJatUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
    return new JatUncaughtExceptionHandler(this);
}
```

- `getJatUncaughtExceptionHandler()` — catches uncaught exceptions on the JavaFX Application Thread.

- Exception handler of the JRebirth Internal Thread

```java
@Override
protected UncaughtExceptionHandler getJitUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
    return new JitUncaughtExceptionHandler(this);
}
```

- `getJitUncaughtExceptionHandler()` — catches uncaught exceptions on JIT (messaging thread).

- Default Exception handler of all other threads

```java
@Override
protected UncaughtExceptionHandler getDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
    return new DefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler(this);
}
```

- `getDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler()` — fallback for JTP slots and other pooled threads.



## Initialization Phases

- [init] (#Init_Phase)
- [start] (#Start_Phase)


### Init Phase

The Initialization phase is composed by:

- 00-10% \- Begin Initialization (Loading Resources transition)
- 10-30% \- Customizable Pre-Init Steps
- 30-40% \- Loading Message Files
- 40-50% \- Loading Configuration Files
- 50-60% \- Attach Exception Handler and Prepare the JIT
- 60-70% \- Preload Fonts and attach CSS Files
- 70-90% \- Customizable Pre-Init Steps
- 100% \- trigger the start phase

Customizable steps are handled by 2 methods to override:


```java
@Override
protected void preInit() {
    notifyPreloader(new ProgressNotification(0.15));
    // custom work between 10% and 30% of boot progress
    notifyPreloader(new ProgressNotification(200)); // custom message id
}
```

- `preInit()` — runs during init phase (10–30% progress); use for early custom setup.
- `notifyPreloader(ProgressNotification(double))` — fine-grained bar update (0.11–0.29).
- `notifyPreloader(ProgressNotification(int))` — message id understood by your preloader (e.g. 200, 300).


```java
@Override
protected void postInit() {
    notifyPreloader(new ProgressNotification(0.75));
    // custom work between 70% and 90% of boot progress
    notifyPreloader(new ProgressNotification(800)); // custom message id
}
```

- `postInit()` — runs late in init (70–90% progress), after fonts/CSS preload.
- Progress doubles use range 0.71–0.89; message ids 800 or 900 for custom preloader text.


### Start Phase

The Start phase will build and attach the scene object.
Then it will start the **JRebirth Thread** (JIT) and show the stage.


# Manage Configuration

JRebirth provides a configuration engine that allow to parse configuration files and inject values into application.


## @Configuration

Your application class can use the dedicated **@Configuration** annotation.
The **AbstractApplication** class use a default one:


```java
@Configuration(".*jrebirth")
```

- Default on `AbstractApplication` — loads `*-jrebirth.properties` from the classpath.
- Matched files inject parameters into the application at startup.


Hereafter you have a full annotation usage:



```java
@Configuration(value = ".*-jrebirth", extension = "properties", schedule = 60)
```

- `value = ".*-jrebirth"` — regex filter; matches files like `myapp-jrebirth.properties`.
- `extension = "properties"` — file suffix without the dot.
- `schedule = 60` — optional reload check interval in seconds (0 = no hot reload).

This annotation has 3 properties:
- value
- extension
- schedule


### Value (default property)

Define the wildcard used to find configuration files.

The format is the same as Regex Pattern (ie: .*-jrebirth => for abc-jrebirth.EXTENSION files, abc matches the .* regex part)

The default value is empty, no search will be done.

### Extension

Define the file extension to find configuration files.

The extension must not included the first dot (ie: properties => for abc-jrebirth.properties files)

The default value is "properties" to load properties files


### Schedule

Define the delay used to check if the file has changed in order to reload configuration files.

This value is in seconds.

The default value is 0, no check will be done (this feature is still under development)



## Avoid Configuration

It's possible to avoid configuration mechanism, for example if you want to use your own and don't need another process at start-up.

You can disable it by setting an empty **@Configuration** annotation.


```java
@Configuration
```

- Empty `@Configuration` — disables automatic configuration file scanning.


# Manage Localization

JRebirth provides a Internationalization engine that allow to localize internal resources and also your resources.
It parses properties files and inject values into application.


## @Localized

Your application class can use the dedicated **@Localized** annotation.
The **AbstractApplication** class use a default one:


```java
@Localized(".*_rb")
```

- `@Localized(".*_rb")` — loads `MyMessages_rb.properties`, `MyMessages_fr_rb.properties`, etc.
- Place message bundles on the classpath; keys become available through the resource engine.


This annotation has 2 properties:
- value
- schedule

### Value (default property)

Define the wildcard used to find configuration files.

The format is the same as Regex Pattern (ie: .*_rb => for MyMessages_rb.properties files, MyMessages matches the .* regex part).

The default value is empty, no search will be done.


### Schedule

Define the delay used to check if the file has changed in order to reload properties files.

This value is in seconds.

The default value is 0, no check will be done (this feature is still under development)


## Avoid Localization

It's possible to avoid localization mechanism, for example if you want to improve performance by avoiding any translation into logs.

You can disable it by setting an empty **@Localized** annotation.


```java
@Localized
```

- Empty `@Localized` — disables i18n bundle scanning (useful for headless or performance-sensitive apps).

